# OpenCode iOS port patch -- 0012
# Title: iPhoneOS pure-JS line REPL for `opencode` / `opencode mini` (no OpenTUI/FFI)
# Upstream: anomalyco/opencode @ e8238bae9b807b72ecfc75afd3a9fe2e6b07b944 (v2.2.5)
# Requires: patches 0001-0011 applied first.
# Files: packages/cli/src/ios-repl.ts (new), packages/cli/src/util/ios.ts,
#        packages/cli/src/commands/handlers/default.ts, packages/cli/src/commands/handlers/mini.ts,
#        packages/cli/src/run/run.ts
# Why: the real TUI cannot run on iPhoneOS. @opentui/core 0.8.0 loads its Zig native core through
#      node:ffi, which needs Node.js >= 26.4 with --experimental-ffi; the device-verified iPhoneOS
#      runtime is Node 22.9 (no node:ffi, `bad option: --experimental-ffi`). An iPhoneOS OpenTUI
#      build could never be loaded, so patch 0011 only made the failure clean. This patch gives
#      iPhone users a usable interactive experience in pure JS:
#      - In a terminal, `opencode` (no args) and `opencode mini` start a node:readline line REPL
#        instead of the TUI. It never imports @opencode/tui/OpenTUI.
#      - Each line goes through the existing non-interactive run pipeline (same as `opencode run`),
#        so streamed assistant text, tool activity lines, auto-handled permission/questions and
#        Ctrl-C interruption behave identically; session continuity across turns is like run -c.
#      - /help, /new, /exit and Ctrl-D; unknown /commands print help; errors return to the prompt
#        instead of ending the process; stdin is no longer consumed by the run pipeline (REPL owns
#        it via OPENCODE_RUN_SKIP_STDIN).
#      - Non-TTY invocations keep the guidance + exit 1 (message now explains the Node >= 26.4 +
#        node:ffi requirement, the TTY requirement and the line-REPL/headless alternatives).
#      - OPENCODE_IOS_REPL_ASSUME_TTY=1 is a host-test hook to exercise the REPL with piped stdio.
# Test: OPENCODE_IOS_REPL_ASSUME_TTY=1 node opencode.mjs with piped /help /new /exit lines on host
#       Node 18.20.4 (verified locally); device PTY run with a mock provider does the real E2E.
# Status: iOS-only path; non-iOS behavior is unchanged.

